.TH "MEDIASTREAMTRACK.GETSETTINGS" "3JS" "October 12, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
MediaStreamTrack.getSettings \- MediaStreamTrack: getSettings() method
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[B]\f[CB]getSettings()\f[B]\f[R] method of the
\f[CR]MediaStreamTrack\f[R] interface returns an object containing the
current values of each of the constrainable properties for the current
\f[CR]MediaStreamTrack\f[R].
.PP
See Capabilities, constraints, and settings for details on how to work
with constrainable properties.
.SH SYNTAX
.IP
.EX
getSettings()
.EE
.SS Parameters
None.
.SS Return value
An object describing the current configuration of the track\(cqs
constrainable properties.
.RS
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] The returned object identifies the current values of
every constrainable property, including those which are platform
defaults rather than having been expressly set by the site\(cqs code.
To instead fetch the most\-recently established constraints for the
track\(cqs properties, as specified by the site\(cqs code, use
\f[CR]getConstraints()\f[R].
.RE
.PP
These values will adhere as closely as possible to any constraints
previously described using a \f[CR]MediaTrackConstraints\f[R] object and
set using \f[CR]applyConstraints()\f[R], and will adhere to the default
constraints for any properties whose constraints haven\(cqt been
changed, or whose customized constraints couldn\(cqt be matched.
This lets you determine what value was selected to comply with your
specified constraints for each property value provided when calling
either \f[CR]getUserMedia()\f[R] or
\f[CR]MediaStreamTrack.applyConstraints()\f[R].
.PP
Certain listed properties may not be included in the object, either
because they\(cqre not supported by the browser or because they\(cqre
not available due to context.
.PP
For example, because RTP doesn\(cqt provide some of these values during
negotiation of a WebRTC connection, a track associated with a
\f[CR]RTCPeerConnection\f[R] will not include the following:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]deviceId\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]groupId\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]echoCancellation\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]latency\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]facingMode\f[R]
.SS Properties of all media tracks
.TP
\f[B]deviceId\f[R]
A string uniquely identifying the source of the corresponding
\f[CR]MediaStreamTrack\f[R] for the origin corresponding to the browsing
session.
This ID is valid across multiple browsing sessions for the same origin
and is guaranteed to be different for all other origins, so you can
safely use it to request that the same source be used for multiple
sessions, for example.
.RS
.PP
All tracks with the same source will share the same ID for any given
origin, so \f[CR]MediaStreamTrack.getCapabilities()\f[R] will always
return exactly one value for \f[CR]deviceId\f[R].
That makes the device ID not useful for any changes to constraints when
calling \f[CR]MediaStreamTrack.applyConstraints()\f[R].
It can, however, be used for initially selecting media when calling
\f[CR]MediaDevices.getUserMedia()\f[R].
.RS
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] An exception to the rule that device IDs are the same
across browsing sessions: private browsing mode will use a different ID,
and will change it each browsing session.
.RE
.PP
The actual value of the string is determined by the source of the track,
and there is no guarantee what form it will take, although the
specification does recommend it be a \c
.UR https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Universally_unique_identifier
GUID
.UE \c
\&.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]groupId\f[R]
A string uniquely identifying the group of devices that includes the
source for the \f[CR]MediaStreamTrack\f[R].
Two devices (as identified by the \f[CR]deviceId\f[R]) are considered
part of the same group if they are from the same physical device.
For instance, the audio input and output devices for the speaker and
microphone built into a phone would share the same group ID, since
they\(cqre part of the same physical device.
The microphone on a headset would have a different ID, though.
.RS
.PP
This ID is browsing\-session unique and is not usable across multiple
browsing sessions.
However, it can be used when calling \f[CR]getUserMedia()\f[R] to ensure
that different tracks use or don\(cqt use the same device\(emsuch as
using the same headset for input and output.
There is no situation in which the groupId is useful when calling
\f[CR]applyConstraints()\f[R], since the value can\(cqt be changed.
.PP
The actual value of the string is determined by the source of the track,
and there is no guarantee what form it will take, although the
specification does recommend it be a GUID.
.RE
.SS Properties of audio tracks
.TP
\f[B]autoGainControl\f[R]
A boolean specifying whether automatic gain control (AGC) is enabled.
Automatic gain control allows a sound source to manage source media
volume changes automatically to maintain a steady overall volume level.
This feature is typically used on microphones, although other input
sources can also provide it.
.TP
\f[B]channelCount\f[R]
An integer specifying the number of audio channels present on the track
(therefore indicating how many audio samples exist in each audio frame).
This is 1 for mono, 2 for stereo, and so forth.
.TP
\f[B]echoCancellation\f[R]
A boolean specifying whether echo cancellation is enabled.
Echo cancellation attempts to prevent echo effects on a two\-way audio
connection by reducing or eliminating crosstalk between the user\(cqs
input and output devices.
For example, it might apply a filter that negates the sound produced by
the speakers from being included in the microphone\(cqs generated input
track.
.TP
\f[B]latency\f[R]
A floating point number specifying the audio latency, in seconds.
Latency is the amount of time that elapses between the start of
processing the audio and the data being available to the next step in
the audio utilization process.
This value is a target value; actual latency may vary to some extent for
various reasons, including CPU, transmission, and storage overhead.
.TP
\f[B]noiseSuppression\f[R]
A boolean specifying whether noise suppression is enabled.
Noise suppression automatically filters the audio to remove background
noise, equipment hum, etc., from the sound before delivering it to your
code.
This feature is typically used on microphones, although other input
sources can also provide it.
.TP
\f[B]restrictOwnAudio\f[R]
A boolean specifying whether the browser will attempt to filter out
system audio originating from the capturing tab during screen capture.
For example, if the capturing web page itself is playing embedded audio
or video, that audio would be included in the capture.
Since this could lead to an undesirable echo or interfere with the
intended audio sources from other tabs or applications, removing it from
the capture is desirable.
If audio removal via processing fails, the user agent may exclude all
audio originating from the capturing tab.
.RS
.RS
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] If the captured display surface doesn\(cqt include
system audio, this setting will have no effect.
.RE
.RE
.TP
\f[B]sampleRate\f[R]
An integer specifying the sample rate in samples per second of the audio
data.
Common values include 44,100 (standard CD audio), 48,000 (standard
digital audio), 96,000 (commonly used in audio mastering and
post\-production), and 192,000 (used for high\-resolution audio in
professional recording and mastering sessions).
However, lower values are often used to reduce bandwidth requirements;
8,000 samples per second is adequate for comprehensible albeit imperfect
human speech, and both 11,025 and 22,050 are often used for
low\-bandwidth, reduced quality sound and music.
.TP
\f[B]sampleSize\f[R]
An integer specifying the linear size, in bits, of each audio sample.
The most commonly used sample size is 16 bits per sample, which is used
for CD audio, etc.
Other common sample sizes are 8 (for reduced bandwidth requirements) and
24 (for high\-resolution professional audio).
.RS
.PP
Each audio channel on the track requires \f[CR]sampleSize\f[R] bits.
That means that a given sample actually uses (\f[CR]sampleSize\f[R] / 8)
* \f[CR]channelCount\f[R] bytes of data.
For example, 16\-bit stereo audio requires (16/8)*2 or 4 bytes per
sample.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]suppressLocalAudioPlayback\f[R]
A boolean specifying whether the audio playing in a tab will stop
playing from a user\(cqs local speakers when the tab is captured.
For example, when broadcasting a video call to an external AV system in
a conference room, you will want the audio to play out of the AV system,
not the local speakers.
This way, the audio will be louder and clearer, and also in sync with
the conference video.
.TP
\f[B]volume\f[R] \f[I](deprecated)\f[R] \f[I](non\-standard)\f[R]
A floating point number specifying the volume level of the track.
This value will be between 0.0 (silent) to 1.0 (maximum supported volume
for the device).
.SS Properties of video tracks
.TP
\f[B]aspectRatio\f[R]
A floating point number specifying the width of the image in pixels
divided by its height in pixels, accurate to at least 10 decimal places.
Common values include 1.3333333333 (for the classic television 4:3
\(lqstandard\(rq aspect ratio, also used on tablets such as Apple\(cqs
iPad), 1.7777777778 (for the 16:9 high\-definition widescreen aspect
ratio), and 1.6 (for the 16:10 aspect ratio common among widescreen
computers and tablets).
.TP
\f[B]facingMode\f[R]
A string specifying the direction the camera is facing.
The value will be one of:
.RS
.TP
\f[B]\(dquser\(dq\f[R]
The video source is facing toward the user (commonly known as a
\(lqselfie cam\(rq); this includes, for example, the front\-facing
camera on a smartphone.
.TP
\f[B]\(dqenvironment\(dq\f[R]
The video source is facing away from the user, thereby viewing their
environment.
This is the back camera on a smartphone.
This is typically the highest quality camera on the device, used for
general photography.
.TP
\f[B]\(dqleft\(dq\f[R]
The video source is facing toward the user but to their left, such as a
camera aimed toward the user but over their left shoulder.
.TP
\f[B]\(dqright\(dq\f[R]
The video source is facing toward the user but to their right, such as a
camera aimed toward the user but over their right shoulder.
These may represent separate cameras, or they may represent directions
in which an adjustable camera can be pointed.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]frameRate\f[R]
A floating point number specifying how many frames of video per second
the track includes.
If the value can\(cqt be determined for any reason, the value will match
the vertical sync rate of the device the user agent is running on.
.TP
\f[B]height\f[R]
An integer specifying the height of the track\(cqs video data in pixels.
.TP
\f[B]width\f[R]
An integer value specifying the width of the track\(cqs video data in
pixels.
.TP
\f[B]resizeMode\f[R]
A string specifying the mode used by the user agent to derive the
resolution of the track.
The value will be one of:
.RS
.TP
\f[B]\(dqnone\(dq\f[R]
The track has the resolution offered by the camera, its driver or the
OS.
.TP
\f[B]\(dqcrop\-and\-scale\(dq\f[R]
The track\(cqs resolution might be the result of the user agent using
cropping or downscaling from a higher camera resolution.
.RE
.SS Properties of shared screen tracks
Tracks containing video shared from a user\(cqs screen (regardless of
whether the screen data comes from the entire screen or a portion of a
screen, like a window or tab) are generally treated like video tracks,
except that they also support the following settings:
.TP
\f[B]cursor\f[R]
A string specifying whether or not the mouse cursor is being included in
the generated stream and under what conditions.
Possible values are:
.RS
.TP
\f[B]always\f[R]
The mouse is always visible in the video content of the
\f[CR]MediaStream\f[R], unless the mouse has moved outside the area of
the content.
.TP
\f[B]motion\f[R]
The mouse cursor is always included in the video if it\(cqs moving, and
for a short time after it stops moving.
.TP
\f[B]never\f[R]
The mouse cursor is never included in the shared video.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]displaySurface\f[R]
A string specifying the type of source the track contains.
The value will be one of:
.RS
.TP
\f[B]browser\f[R]
The stream\(cqs video track presents the entire contents of a single
browser tab that the user selected during the
\f[CR]getDisplayMedia()\f[R] call.
.TP
\f[B]monitor\f[R]
The stream\(cqs video track presents the complete contents of one or
more of the user\(cqs screens.
Any empty space (if the displays are of different dimensions) is filled
with a backdrop chosen by the user agent.
.TP
\f[B]window\f[R]
The stream\(cqs video track presents the contents of a single window
selected by the user.
The window may be from any application, not necessarily just from within
the user agent.
Not all user agents support all of these surface types.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]logicalSurface\f[R]
A boolean specifying whether the display area being captured is a
logical surface.
Logical surfaces are not necessarily entirely onscreen, or may even be
off\-screen, such as windows\(cq backing buffers (where only part of the
buffer is visible without scrolling the containing window) and offscreen
rendering contexts.
A visible display surface (that is, a surface for which
\f[CR]logicalSurface\f[R] returns \f[CR]false\f[R]) is the portion of a
logical display surface that is currently visible onscreen.
.RS
.PP
The most common scenario in which a display surface may be a logical one
is if the selected surface contains the entire content area of a window
which is too large to display onscreen at once.
Since the window that contains the surface has to be scrolled to show
the rest of the contents, the surface is a logical one.
.PP
For example, a user agent \f[I]may\f[R] choose to allow the user to
choose whether to share the entire document (a \f[CR]browser\f[R] with
\f[CR]logicalSurface\f[R] value of \f[CR]true\f[R]), or just the
currently visible portion of the document (where the
\f[CR]logicalSurface\f[R] of the \f[CR]browser\f[R] surface is
\f[CR]false\f[R]).
.RE
.TP
\f[B]screenPixelRatio\f[R]
A number representing the ratio between the physical resolution and the
logical resolution.
It cannot be used as a constraint or capability.
The value is calculated by dividing the size of a CSS pixel at a page
zoom of \f[CR]1.0\f[R] and using a scale factor of \f[CR]1.0\f[R] on the
capturing screen by the vertical size of a pixel from the captured
display surface.
.RS
.PP
It is common for a screen to have zoom applied via the operating system
(OS), for example when the display is a high\-resolution display, and
you want the graphics to be shown at the same physical size as they
would on a standard resolution display.
The resolution before applying the zoom is called the \f[B]logical
resolution\f[R], and the resolution after the zoom is applied is called
the \f[B]physical resolution\f[R].
.PP
For example:
.IP \(bu 2
If the captured display surface is being displayed on a standard
resolution screen where physical pixel dimensions are about the same as
CSS pixel dimensions, \f[CR]screenPixelRatio\f[R] will return a value of
\f[CR]1\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
If, however, the captured display surface is being displayed on a
high\-dpi resolution screen where physical pixel dimensions are about
half the CSS pixel dimensions (so each CSS pixel uses 4 physical
pixels), \f[CR]screenPixelRatio\f[R] will return a value of
\f[CR]2\f[R].
.PP
If the sender\(cqs captured screen is zoomed in, then the physical
resolution is greater than the logical resolution, and a
video\-conferencing app can therefore save bandwidth and CPU by:
.IP "1." 3
Removing the zoom applied to the captured display surface by the OS.
.IP "2." 3
Sending the video of the screen capture at the logical resolution.
.IP "3." 3
Reapplying the zoom after receiving it on the remote client to size it
back up to its physical resolution.
.PP
This property allows applications using the Screen Capture API to save
resources by sending the video of a screen capture at its logical, or
device independent, resolution.
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Basic \f[CR]screenPixelRatio\f[R] usage
In this example, the application defines a constant
\f[CR]RESOLUTION_LIMIT\f[R], which represents the scaling factor beyond
which the sending application should send video at the logical
resolution rather than the physical resolution.
.PP
When \f[CR]screenPixelRatio\f[R] exceeds this limit, the application
uses the \f[CR]screenPixelRatio\f[R] value to calculate the logical
resolution from the physical resolution, and then constrains the
captured \f[CR]MediaStreamTrack\f[R] to the logical resolution.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] RESOLUTION_LIMIT = 1.5;

\f[B]async\f[R] \f[B]function\f[R] startCapture() {
  \f[B]const\f[R] stream = \f[B]await\f[R] navigator.mediaDevices.getDisplayMedia({
    video: \f[B]true\f[R],
  });
  \f[B]const\f[R] track = stream.getVideoTracks()[0];
  \f[B]const\f[R] settings = track.getSettings();
  \f[B]const\f[R] capabilities = track.getCapabilities();

  \f[B]if\f[R] (settings.screenPixelRatio > RESOLUTION_LIMIT) {
    \f[B]const\f[R] physicalWidth = capabilities.width.max;
    \f[B]const\f[R] physicalHeight = capabilities.height.max;
    \f[B]const\f[R] logicalWidth = physicalWidth / settings.screenPixelRatio;
    \f[B]const\f[R] logicalHeight = physicalHeight / settings.screenPixelRatio;
    \f[B]await\f[R] track.applyConstraints({
      width: logicalWidth,
      height: logicalHeight,
    });
  }
}
.EE
